**IaC Snippet for Emergency Cross-Account Trust Role:**

```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "emergency_sap_role" {
  name = "SAP-Emergency-Response-Role"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow",
      Principal = {
        AWS = "arn:aws:iam::PROD-ACCOUNT:root"
      },
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole",
      Condition = {
        Bool = { "aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent" = "true" }
      }
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_vpc_endpoint_service" "sap_privatelink" {
  acceptance_required = true
  network_load_balancer_arns = [aws_lb.sap_nlb.arn]
  allowed_principals = [aws_iam_role.emergency_sap_role.arn]
}
```

**Critical Success Factors:**
- All actions must be logged to CloudTrail with immutable audit trails
- Emergency role assumptions require separate out-of-band approval for production access
- JIT elevation tokens must have strict TTLs (typically 1-4 hours) with automatic expiration
- Key rotation must maintain backward compatibility during transition periods?

## Why This Is Asked

Addresses real-world needs for analytics while protecting sensitive SAP data. Evaluates understanding of data masking, per-tenant access control, and end-to-end data lineage.

## Key Concepts

- SAP data export pipelines to data lakes
- Field-level masking for PII/financial data
- Deterministic masking to preserve join feasibility
- IAM SCPs and per-tenant roles for isolation
- Data lineage via Glue Data Catalog
- KMS encryption at rest; minimal IaC for keys/roles

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: deterministic mask preserving format
function maskForColumn(val){ 
  if (val===null) return null;
  const s = String(val);
  const masked = s.replace(/[0-9]/g,'0');
  return masked.length>0 ? masked : val;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotence and integrity after multiple masking runs?
- What strategies ensure masking policies stay in sync with SAP authorization changes?
